  "total_cost_usd": 0.486433,
  "subtype": "success",
  "final_text": "I submitted **part-based** with confidence 0.75. I used 53 of the 60 budget.\n\n**What I did:**\n1. I trained the learner on three tasks: L1-S1-M1, L2-S2-M2 and L3-S3-M3. Rules L4, S4 and M4 stayed unseen.\n2. I tested other tasks several times each, since a single test result varies a lot (standard deviation about 12 examples).\n3. For the deciding step I trained L4-S4-M4. It gives the same response as L1-S4-M4 on none of the 256 inputs, but it contains the two rules L1-S4-M4 was missing.\n\n**Results (average examples needed to reach criterion):**\n\n| Task | Rules new to the learner | Before training L4-S4-M4 | After |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L1-S4-M4 | 2, then 0 | 37 (4 tests) | 14 (10 tests) |\n| L4-S2-M2 | 1, then 0 | 26 (3 tests) | 25 (7 tests) |\n| Tasks with all rules familiar | 0 | about 21 (9 tests) | \u2013 |\n| Tasks with 2\u20133 new rules | 2\u20133 | about 35 | \u2013 |\n\n**Why part-based:** L1-S4-M4 got about 23 examples cheaper after training a task that matches none of its outputs. That drop is large compared with the noise. Reusing stored rules explains it directly. A learner that stores whole tasks and judges similarity by matching outputs should have gained nothing from that training.\n\n**Why not higher confidence:**\n- Tasks with one new rule cost about the same as tasks with none (about 22 vs 21). That is not what a part-based learner would be expected to show.\n- L4-S2-M2 should also have got cheaper once its last new rule was trained, and it didn't.\n- I can't rule out a holistic learner whose similarity measure counts shared rule labels rather than matching outputs. L1-S4-M4 shares two labels with L4-S4-M4, so such a learner would also predict the drop.",
